  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Last Sanding Pass",
    "overview": "The page is a stairwell under construction: dark violet-slate sections are the stairwell in shadow, light sections are sanded joint compound, and the two meet on slow, feathered mud edges instead of straight lines. The logo supplies the only color: violet for action, brushed silver for lettering. The hero wall is a working surface you sand by hand.\n\nDensity is phone-first and generous: one column, 56px buttons, 72px service rows, 16px gutters. Hierarchy comes from weight and case alone: heavy uppercase headings over plain sentence-case body, all in the system font stack (a deliberate house choice, not a placeholder). Depth lives in the canvas lighting, not in UI shadows.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Deep violet-slate ground, never black; compound-white light sections.",
      "Feathered, moving mud edges at every dark/light seam.",
      "One violet action color; a separate blue used only inside the form.",
      "System font stack: 900-weight uppercase headings, 400 sentence-case body.",
      "Flat UI; window light from the top left drives all depth in the wall."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Never-Black Rule",
        "body": "The darkest surface is Stairwell Shadow (violet-slate). No #000 or near-black grounds.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Accents Rule",
        "body": "Violet means \"act\" (fill, arrow, checked). Blue means \"you are here in the form\" (hover/focus). Never swap them, and never use violet as the form's hover color.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hover-Only Accent Rule",
        "body": "Arrows and links sit neutral at rest and take the accent on hover or focus.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Caps-Are-Lettering Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase belongs to headings, the brand lockup and the sign-off, which mirror the logo. Body, labels, buttons and service names stay sentence case.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Thumb Rule",
        "body": "Every tap target is 44px or taller; primary buttons are 56px and full width on phones.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Window-Light Rule",
        "body": "Light comes from the top left. Highlights go up-left, shadows down-right (ridges, spots, the sponge's drop shadow `rgba(40,26,70,.38)` offset 5/7px). Anything new drawn in the wall follows the same light.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mud Edge Rule",
        "body": "Dark and light sections never meet on a straight line. Each seam is a 44px SVG with two layers: a gradient feather (opacity 0, .35, .7) at baseline 12 and a solid fill at baseline 25. Each follows three layered sine waves (`SPEED` 0.0016, `AMP` 7), pauses off screen, and holds still under reduced motion. Set `data-from` / `data-to` to the two grounds.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** join every dark/light section with a mud edge (Mud Edge Rule).",
      "**Do** keep violet for action and Estimate Blue for form hover/focus only (Two Accents Rule).",
      "**Do** change only the arrow on service-row hover and focus.",
      "**Do** light anything drawn in the wall from the top left (Window-Light Rule).",
      "**Do** run new motion through the shared `Loop` (one rAF, paused off screen, still under reduced motion), with tunables as named constants at the top of its IIFE.",
      "**Do** keep the system font stack; build hierarchy with weight (400/800/900) and case."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use black or near-black grounds; Stairwell Shadow is the floor.",
      "**Don't** add thin highlight lines or sheens to UI surfaces or photos. The white ridge highlights inside the wall canvas are the material under raking light, not decoration, and stay.",
      "**Don't** give buttons, rows or inputs shadows; only work photos are lifted.",
      "**Don't** style non-interactive labels as chips. Chip styling means a real form checkbox.",
      "**Don't** put captions under the gallery photos or an intro line above them."
    ]
  }
}